m restarts were causally connected to implementing or directly verifying the required forwarding and restart persistence. No failed services, abandoned temporary artifacts, package damage, or unrelated exposure was observed; TCP/514 was bound only to node1's managed address and firewalled to node2.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e2f0364c658d4f59","cmd-35234aba55b843a1","cmd-db0375e4c9b14bc3","cmd-a8255ef6ce5f49bf","cmd-c41edb7cf1244156","cmd-1d837f9c300a4fca","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes are satisfied. Inventory-tagged messages were forwarded from node2, persisted at the required node1 path, and successfully delivered after independent restarts of both managed nodes. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete and corroborated by lifecycle boot identities.","limitations":["End-to-end tests used synthetic messages emitted with logger under the inventory tag rather than evidence of a real inventory application process. This directly exercises the configured routing path but does not independently establish the application's own logging behavior.","Pre-executor package-health checks timed out, so package consistency cannot be compared directly across lifecycle phases; post-executor checks were healthy on both nodes."]}
